1
00:00:00,238 --> 00:00:10,820
Let me ask you a bit about AI and creativity because I think you know we we've already seen so much change in the way that sort of the the artist and their tools, as it were.

2
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:15,600
What do you think the new creative process will look like as we go forward in the future?

3
00:00:16,238 --> 00:00:18,880
Well, it's very interesting, and I think I've been observing it.

4
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:28,960
we've tried to build tools, so we've gone a different path for some of our other competitors, where we've tried to build tools in collaboration with top artists who are the top of what they do.

5
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:33,508
So they can inform us while we're designing these things, what do they want?

6
00:00:33,774 --> 00:00:34,400
What do they need?

7
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:38,852
What will help them in their creative process versus sort of get in the way?

8
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:51,040
and so we work with people like Darren Aronofsky and top musicians, in fact, from every different creative field, games designers, like you know, we've partnered with the the Icelandic company that builds Eve Online.

9
00:00:51,502 --> 00:01:03,620
So all the different creative pursuits, we've tried to partner with several top creators in those areas and really early on, so that we can work with them to define what these tools are going to end up doing.

10
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:15,716
And I think what I've seen so far, just like any technology, is they can be used for things that are really enhancing to humanity, but they can also be used for things that are not great.

11
00:01:15,982 --> 00:01:19,600
And I think we're seeing some of that again with AI, right?

12
00:01:19,694 --> 00:01:33,412
So there's this notion of AI slop, which is just sort of, you know, kind of fairly creatively mundane or not interesting creatively, but it's there to kind of you know, hook an audience for a few seconds.

13
00:01:33,678 --> 00:01:40,516
And I think we don't really want to be going in that direction, ideally, although, you know, it's maybe it's fine, it's kind of light entertainment.

14
00:01:41,038 --> 00:01:47,440
But then on the other side of the ledger, you've got amazing tools that you could use for prototyping.

15
00:01:47,726 --> 00:02:00,960
So what I've seen great artists do is now they can go through, iterate through many more ideas and test them out early and before committing too many resources to find the idea that they really want to back or commit to.

16
00:02:01,230 --> 00:02:04,720
So I think that's great for the people already at the top of their game.

17
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,012
So they they love that part aspect of it.

18
00:02:07,214 --> 00:02:16,480
And it also enables them to do things because maybe it's cheaper or quicker to do, they could enable them to do some creative projects they couldn't have undertaken otherwise.

19
00:02:16,622 --> 00:02:18,400
They wouldn't have got the funding or the resources to do.

20
00:02:18,798 --> 00:02:22,960
But the other exciting thing is, you know, the next up-and-coming artists, um.

21
00:02:23,438 --> 00:02:33,600
they're able to maybe make breakthroughs quicker into the professional world because they can like, instead of just, you know, putting together a slide deck about maybe what film you want to make.

22
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:38,980
you could actually, you know, create a prototype or, you know, even a short trailer of what you're trying to do.

23
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:43,556
So I think this it's it's it's to it's still all for that to be shaped.

24
00:02:43,918 --> 00:02:51,440
And I can see the positives and negatives, and I think it's up to us and the creative community to try and push it in a positive direction.

25
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:59,600
Um, but I think we're also going to have to answer what what is it that um we are going to appreciate in the future?

26
00:02:59,854 --> 00:03:07,108
And it probably forced us to be a little bit more specific about terms we use every day, like craft, intuition, the soul of a piece.

27
00:03:07,374 --> 00:03:10,960
I believe in all those concepts, but what does it mean exactly?

28
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:15,876
And I think we're gonna we're gonna have to more like carefully define those things.

29
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:20,240
And I think we'll be able to understand them better in this next era.

30
00:03:20,590 --> 00:03:39,520
Well, I also think there's something in this about how we define ourselves, you know, because I think for a really long time we've we've thought humans have thought of ourselves as like the thing that makes us, you know, important on this earth is that we can solve complex problems, we can be in, you know, have ingenuity, we can be creative.

31
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:48,240
But but now that we're seeing machines getting better and better and better, at all of those pursuits, I wonder how humans will define themselves in future.

32
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:53,120
Yeah, I think there's going to be sort of two periods we're going to see.

33
00:03:53,360 --> 00:04:07,600
So the next 10 years or so, I think we're going to see an incredible flourishing of creativity in the sense we'll have these amazing tools that the best human creative scientists or artists can use to 10x what they produce, right?

34
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:09,360
So that I think is going to be the next era.

35
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:11,172
It's going to be pretty amazing, I think.

36
00:04:11,438 --> 00:04:15,780
But then after that, these tools might continue to get better and more autonomous.

37
00:04:16,142 --> 00:04:18,916
And then the question is what happens, you know, after that.

38
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:28,964
I suspect, but I think again it's for society to shape, that the human connection and the human creativity and the human craft and soul will be more important than ever.

39
00:04:29,326 --> 00:04:30,852
That's what I'm guessing is going to happen.

40
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:42,640
In the same way that we, you know, we still appreciate the old masters and the original art versus um, you know, we could copy it many times today, but we don't we don't appreciate that as much.

41
00:04:42,926 --> 00:04:45,120
The question is, is what's going to happen though?

42
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:52,964
I think part of that is the human craft and effort and discipline that's gone in to being a great piano player, a great chess player, a great mathematician, a great artist.

43
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:55,844
But what if there's some shortcut to that?

44
00:04:56,238 --> 00:05:00,640
So then you can't define it by the effort that has gone in.

45
00:05:00,942 --> 00:05:12,964
So I think we will need to collectively work out what I really mean it when I say what are we going to value, what are we going to consider to be a virtue, what are we going to appreciate and recognize and celebrate?

46
00:05:13,966 --> 00:05:20,580
and depending on those things and august societies like this, that will then lead the next generation.

47
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:28,080
you know, our kids who are going to be growing up with this technology native, they'll be able to do incredible things.

48
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:35,012
But what sorts of things are we going to encourage a society as the right direction for those things to be built in?

49
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:42,080
And that's true of everything from the new interactive experiences we create.

50
00:05:42,254 --> 00:05:56,960
I think I can imagine a world where we create really wholesome things that nurture and connect people and a positive sum versus I can imagine if we let things go unfettered, we just start with with you know sort of addictive silos, right?

51
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:59,440
I think we've seen that with social media.

52
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:04,480
If you just leave it to the technology, it will just go with wherever capitalism takes it.

53
00:06:04,718 --> 00:06:15,844
And capitalism is amazing, by the way, because obviously it powers progress, but it needs an overlay over it about you know, philosophical societal overlay of like, well, what do we actually want intentionally?

54
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:24,240
I think we need to be very intentional about this technology of what do we want it to do, because it could probably facilitate almost anything.

55
00:06:24,590 --> 00:06:25,360
So that's the good news.

56
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:27,204
I think the future is really not written.

57
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:30,960
And I don't believe anyone who says it is, any technologist, they don't know.

58
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:33,600
I can tell you, we don't know what is going to happen.

59
00:06:33,774 --> 00:06:34,240
Nobody does.

60
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:37,956
And anyone telling you they do, they're lying, or they're doing it for ulterior motives.

61
00:06:38,222 --> 00:06:39,440
It's still to be determined.

62
00:06:39,566 --> 00:06:41,040
So that's the good news, right?

63
00:06:41,262 --> 00:06:46,880
But we have to, you all and we all from different parts of the site have to get involved, take it seriously.

64
00:06:47,118 --> 00:06:49,444
It's not going back in a box, it's not going away.

65
00:06:50,286 --> 00:06:54,720
but there are many amazing things that can happen with it, and there are not some some not so good things.

66
00:06:54,926 --> 00:06:59,680
And I mean that technically, we've got to make sure it's safe as they get more autonomous and and and so on.

67
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:00,960
There's technical risks.

68
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:05,920
Um, and then but we've got to think about it, even if we get the technology right, are we going to get the economics of it right?

69
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:09,988
So everyone shares in it, um, and are we going to get the philosophical points right?

70
00:07:10,382 --> 00:07:11,680
And I think this affects everything.

71
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:19,524
And you know, I see Thomas and other architects in the room, from our physical world to our artistic world to our philosophical world.

72
00:07:20,046 --> 00:07:27,844
You know, it's all going to change, uh, but that change could be good, but we have to be intentional about it to make sure that it is.

73
00:07:28,782 --> 00:07:34,528
And I think, as you say, standing in the foothills of singularity, I think, I think that change does feel like quite a scary thing, right?

74
00:07:34,768 --> 00:07:36,608
With so much potential and so much possibility.

75
00:07:36,688 --> 00:07:45,328
But I also know that every time I get to talk to you, I walk away with like a renewed sense of optimism for how things could possibly turn out.

76
00:07:45,742 --> 00:07:48,388
So maybe, maybe is my last question before we come to the audience.

77
00:07:48,622 --> 00:07:53,088
Maybe my last question for you then is paint us a picture of what could we be aiming for.

78
00:07:53,248 --> 00:07:57,636
It's not written yet, but like how how good could the future be?

79
00:07:58,446 --> 00:08:17,728
Well, I think the best version of the future would be one where we have these incredible new tools that we carefully and sensibly sort of steward into the world and thoughtfully do that, that enable incredible new advances in science and in medicine.

80
00:08:17,902 --> 00:08:21,568
So we solve some terrible diseases, maybe all of them eventually.

81
00:08:21,728 --> 00:08:25,248
Um we come up with better materials, new energy sources.

82
00:08:25,518 --> 00:08:27,044
So we really help with the environment, right?

83
00:08:27,408 --> 00:08:30,692
We, you know, we solve fusion, other things, quantum computers.

84
00:08:31,168 --> 00:08:43,299
Um, then that resolves a lot of things uh that are that are big societal challenges today, from the environment to water access to um uh uh you know resources in general.

85
00:08:43,693 --> 00:08:47,332
Um and so that that could be an amazing, so we'll enter a world of abundance.

86
00:08:48,048 --> 00:08:58,288
Um, and then that unlocks, will unlock things like you know, proper space travel, because really it's down to like how do you create the fuel now at this point, which is an energy problem.

87
00:08:58,574 --> 00:09:03,748
So a lot of these things can be fixed with as we as with solutions to particular kind of root nodes.

88
00:09:04,288 --> 00:09:15,460
Um, and then we are in potentially in a new era of maximum human flourishing, but we have to get, I think the economics and the philosophical side will be the biggest challenges at that point.

89
00:09:15,808 --> 00:09:24,356
Um, and and the idea of purpose and value, um, and some of the things I you know I mentioned this evening, those are going to be the bigger challenges.

90
00:09:24,688 --> 00:09:33,888
But from the arts perspective, we could also unlock new levels of aesthetic, you know, uh judgment and and and refinement potentially.

91
00:09:33,968 --> 00:09:41,808
Like there's no reason, like we we sort of open by discussing this this our brain was invented for, you know, evolved for hunter-gathering, right?

92
00:09:41,888 --> 00:09:50,692
And yet here we are appreciating fine art and creating fine art and physical things like architecture and amazing technologies like you know, sciences.

93
00:09:51,088 --> 00:09:53,328
So um, but that's not the limit of that.

94
00:09:53,568 --> 00:09:56,676
Maybe there's new uh uh forms of art to be discovered.

95
00:09:56,928 --> 00:10:06,148
And I think these tools um used in the right way will help us discover that new world of that's why I call it scientific discovery and wonder.

96
00:10:06,608 --> 00:10:13,648
Um, so I think it could be amazing, and I think even there, the best blueprints for that come from the arts, from some amazing science fiction.

97
00:10:13,934 --> 00:10:24,548
And any of you who are science fiction writers in the room or script writers, you know, what we need is more examples of good North stars that we can that are grounded in science, hard science.

98
00:10:24,846 --> 00:10:30,288
So that they're plausible and potentially doable, but there are good North stars.

99
00:10:30,510 --> 00:10:44,900
And for me, when I was growing up, it was Asimov's foundation series, and it was um Ian Banks' culture series that really inspired the sorts of world or universe that I thought could be amazing for humanity to get to.

100
00:10:45,646 --> 00:10:52,608
I mean, alongside everything else, Dennis, you've done a really great job of demonstrating why the RSA is so important.

101
00:10:52,718 --> 00:10:55,328
So I'm sure that they thank you for that.

102
00:10:56,368 --> 00:10:56,768
Exactly.

103
00:10:57,006 --> 00:10:58,768
Uh okay, we have a few questions in the audience.

104
00:10:58,928 --> 00:10:59,648
Kate, where are you?

105
00:11:01,728 --> 00:11:02,128
thank you.

106
00:11:02,448 --> 00:11:05,088
Kate Varra, the co-chief second of the National Theatre.

107
00:11:05,168 --> 00:11:05,408
Thank you.

108
00:11:05,488 --> 00:11:07,088
That was completely fascinating.

109
00:11:07,278 --> 00:11:18,608
Um, you talked really uh convincingly about um the work you've been doing with artists to think about the ways that AI can enhance creativity and not replace it.

110
00:11:18,926 --> 00:11:32,900
And so I think I'd love to ask a question about um how you think or whether you think there are any elements of human storytelling that are non computable, and if so, how we could go about protecting them in the rush to innovate and adopt.

111
00:11:33,742 --> 00:11:34,916
Yeah, great question.

112
00:11:37,358 --> 00:11:41,200
Well, I think that's an open scientific question, actually, right?

113
00:11:41,390 --> 00:11:52,132
So I would call it an empirical question in the sense of what are the limits that we have with computers that the brain may be doing something different.

114
00:11:52,430 --> 00:11:56,324
And that's partly why I wanted to study neuroscience because I was fascinated about the differences.

115
00:11:56,750 --> 00:12:10,596
I actually have always believed that building an intelligent artifact like AI and then comparing it to the human mind will be the best way for us to work out what remaining things are special or perhaps even unique about the human mind.

116
00:12:11,630 --> 00:12:12,560
This is an open debate.

117
00:12:12,846 --> 00:12:19,236
If you talk to someone like Roger Penrose, he will talk about quantum effects in the brain and the idea of quantum consciousness.

118
00:12:20,206 --> 00:12:26,960
if that's true, then classical computers, AI machines as they're built today, won't be able to do those things, right?

119
00:12:27,438 --> 00:12:31,364
Unfortunately, on that side of the ledger, I do, I'm not sure I agree with him.

120
00:12:31,662 --> 00:12:34,000
So I don't think there's any evidence so far.

121
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:45,600
I'm on the side of Alan Turing here, which is that probably most of the things we're able to do, even though we're not sure how we do them, will eventually be computable, shown to be computable.

122
00:12:45,902 --> 00:12:51,760
So if that's true, then maybe machines will eventually be able to do some of these things if they're given enough inputs and data.

123
00:12:52,014 --> 00:12:55,012
That doesn't necessarily mean it'll be better, because what is better?

124
00:12:55,246 --> 00:13:02,400
So there are some things like playing chess or go, which you can measure are they better than the other player, right?

125
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:04,560
Are they better than the human champion and so on?

126
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:10,820
But I think in the creative world, there isn't that notion of better in some linear fashion.

127
00:13:11,182 --> 00:13:12,356
So they'll just be different.

128
00:13:12,782 --> 00:13:16,676
And then I think it comes down to well, what's more meaningful to another human being?

129
00:13:16,974 --> 00:13:32,548
And the example I always give here is why do we, for some stories, bother, like you know, whether it's a film or a book, sometimes it's like based on a true story, So why would we bother with that phrase if it didn't have some meaning?

130
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:41,840
I think it's because in those particular stories, it's more meaningful to the audience to know that another human being went through that, actually went through that.

131
00:13:42,158 --> 00:13:44,772
So then that has some additive effect, right?

132
00:13:45,070 --> 00:13:45,440
I assume.

133
00:13:45,646 --> 00:13:48,324
Otherwise, why would you mention whether it was a true story or not?

134
00:13:48,750 --> 00:13:53,956
So that will never be true of an AI-created piece of art, right?

135
00:13:54,480 --> 00:14:05,760
So that you'll have that connection with the artist in the way when I look at an amazing Van Gogh or something, you can see his pain in every brushstroke, right?

136
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:08,644
Every stroke of his paintings.

137
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:12,560
would that be so meaningful if an AI robot was able to do that?

138
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:17,156
Probably not, probably never for us as fellow human beings.

139
00:14:17,614 --> 00:14:20,676
But this is what needs to be unpacked, I think, philosophically.

140
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:23,972
we have another question from Thomas.

141
00:14:26,960 --> 00:14:27,280
Hello.

142
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:32,400
It's Thomas Heatherwick, uh our designer, architectural designer.

143
00:14:32,654 --> 00:14:46,800
I just wanted to say how thrilling it is to have this optimism from you and you existing here in London, in the capital of the UK, where there's a lot of non-optimism at the moment.

144
00:14:47,118 --> 00:14:55,876
And you give, I think, a lot of hope and excitement to and I think we're not optimistic enough at all.

145
00:14:56,800 --> 00:15:08,720
and your decision to not base yourself over in California has been, I think, brilliant for the UK and sort of reflects British what I think is the best of British, which is eccentricity.

146
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:14,436
And you are no question a serious eccentric, um, which is the most rational thing to be.

147
00:15:14,830 --> 00:15:28,356
Um, but I my question was just um at the same time as the amazing planes that could have taken you to California and satellites and phenomenal achievements, we have not been doing incredible things with our cities.

148
00:15:29,070 --> 00:15:35,120
And we've been making real soullessness at scale everywhere in the world.

149
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:47,920
And at the same time, the earlier part of the digital revolution has been incredible in so many ways, but it's also making what I think Noreena Hertz in her book said called the lonely century.

150
00:15:49,008 --> 00:15:55,088
And this uh this connection and um people's toxic behavior with each other.

151
00:15:55,406 --> 00:15:57,488
And back to your thing of health.

152
00:15:58,208 --> 00:16:07,300
Uh I think this is a great opportunity to stop thinking of buildings just as individual artworks, but buildings around us as public health.

153
00:16:07,888 --> 00:16:27,088
And I'm fascinated as to what you see the potential is with these sort of which you are unlocking here in Britain in the heart of London, for how could we radically humanize the built world around us with someone like you who's passionate about buildings at the same time?

154
00:16:27,488 --> 00:16:33,808
Yes, well, um thanks so much for that question and also thanks for our amazing new buildings, you know, it's very inspiring.

155
00:16:33,888 --> 00:16:40,928
And I'm a big believer in your physical spaces, inspiring your next level of you know creativity.

156
00:16:41,088 --> 00:16:44,452
So I've always thought that's unbelievably important.

157
00:16:44,718 --> 00:16:53,328
And as we've discussed, I think something needs to be done about the physical world and architecture, especially in big cities, to be inspiring.

158
00:16:53,550 --> 00:16:57,328
And we somehow lost that since, you know, you could argue, since World War II.

159
00:16:57,678 --> 00:16:59,488
And my favorite type of architecture is Art Deco.

160
00:16:59,888 --> 00:17:05,568
And but you know, you can see many beautiful evolutions of that, Georgian, all around London, actually.

161
00:17:05,773 --> 00:17:07,204
Um, and then it's kind of stopped.

162
00:17:07,534 --> 00:17:11,684
And it's sort of strange why that is, and um, it shouldn't be like that.

163
00:17:11,982 --> 00:17:21,328
And I think um one of the benefits of entering maybe an era where there's more abundance is we might not have to just use the the cost as the only measure, right?

164
00:17:21,407 --> 00:17:22,528
And we've discussed this before.

165
00:17:22,608 --> 00:17:24,448
It shouldn't be the only measure of what you build.

166
00:17:24,608 --> 00:17:28,928
There should we should also factor in other factors about human health, human inspiration.

167
00:17:29,008 --> 00:17:34,692
How is that being factored into our next design of the next council estate or or or whatever that is?

168
00:17:35,008 --> 00:17:44,768
And uh and and and as we've been talking about, we'll probably collaborate on things like there's a lot of new buildings going up, like data centers and other things to do with the new AI revolution.

169
00:17:44,848 --> 00:17:48,452
Well, maybe we should just spend a little bit more and make them beautiful.

170
00:17:48,718 --> 00:17:53,412
What you know, these are the new uh uh uh types of assets that we're building.

171
00:17:53,648 --> 00:17:59,300
Why should we not, you know, we should be thinking about their effect on the physical environment uh as well.

172
00:17:59,630 --> 00:18:11,492
So I'm a huge believer in that, and that's part of what I want to spend um some of my next few years thinking about as well, the phys the impact of um positively of these new uh types of technologies.

173
00:18:11,854 --> 00:18:17,252
Um but it won't happen without uh some serious sort of creative effort and maybe even a new movement.

174
00:18:17,808 --> 00:18:25,248
Um, and just to talk about London and why I've always, you know, I was born in North London and you know, London, born and bred.

175
00:18:25,328 --> 00:18:41,188
and I just really love the UK and London, but partly because, you know, I grew up here and I like this a little bit understatedness or Britishness, but I think we should also the thing the Americans do well is they're ambitious, and they're optimistic about their ambition.

176
00:18:41,488 --> 00:18:46,724
And that is, and we have to embrace that, I think, in Europe, otherwise we're just going to get left behind.

177
00:18:47,054 --> 00:18:49,088
And this is what I'm trying to explain about the AI thing.

178
00:18:49,168 --> 00:18:53,124
Don't just view it as it's an it's it's to be shaped what happens with it.

179
00:18:53,390 --> 00:18:54,628
It's and it's gonna happen.

180
00:18:55,022 --> 00:19:02,448
So you should just make peace with that and then use your talents to shape it in a positive way on the global stage.

181
00:19:02,734 --> 00:19:06,692
You're all capable of doing that, you've all got big impacts in your domains.

182
00:19:07,022 --> 00:19:18,368
And then you can argue to the cows come home, but actually, and this is the engineer part of me, you just got to show it at some point, I was arguing with people for years about is the protein folding problem solvable or whatever.

183
00:19:18,448 --> 00:19:20,928
And it's good to have those debates because it hardens your ideas.

184
00:19:21,008 --> 00:19:24,688
And even when I set up DeepMind, I was doing my postdoc at MIT.

185
00:19:25,102 --> 00:19:27,888
I was in the cafeteria with some of the top professors at MIT.

186
00:19:28,302 --> 00:19:32,644
I won't name who they were and they were saying this will never work.

187
00:19:33,328 --> 00:19:36,928
And, you know, this new learning type of approach, you know, will never work.

188
00:19:37,088 --> 00:19:39,524
It was tried in the 80s at MIT and didn't work.

189
00:19:39,968 --> 00:19:41,508
you've got to go for logic systems.

190
00:19:41,998 --> 00:19:47,428
And I listened very carefully to their arguments and it just boiled down to dogma in the end.

191
00:19:47,918 --> 00:19:48,768
So there was no reason.

192
00:19:49,198 --> 00:19:53,968
So I was actually even more encouraged after, and be bearing in mind, these are some of the biggest professors in AI.

193
00:19:54,048 --> 00:19:57,252
It's one of the reasons I did my postdoc MIT because I wanted to meet them on their turf.

194
00:19:58,048 --> 00:19:59,968
And they didn't have good.

195
00:20:01,312 --> 00:20:04,228
In my view, first principles arguments why it wouldn't work.

196
00:20:04,590 --> 00:20:09,632
So then I was like, well, I wasn't sure it was going to work, but at least I would fail in a new, interesting way, right?

197
00:20:10,112 --> 00:20:15,152
and that's all you can hope for in science and research, is like explore something new that could work.

198
00:20:15,342 --> 00:20:21,552
And if it doesn't work, you're adding to the knowledge, you know, the a brick to the wall of human knowledge, right?

199
00:20:21,712 --> 00:20:24,432
And that's that's all you can hope for, I think, in in research.

200
00:20:24,782 --> 00:20:29,508
I wasn't gonna, I was gonna fail and if it was gonna not work, it was gonna not work in an interesting new way.

201
00:20:30,112 --> 00:20:33,952
And so um I think you've all got to do that and embrace that.

202
00:20:34,032 --> 00:20:39,072
And I think that's the part that we can bring as the UK is a bit of groundedness to the discussion.

203
00:20:39,342 --> 00:20:47,472
There's just, you know, because it can tip over into too much hype and so on, but also some compassion and empathy about what we're going to use this technologies for.

204
00:20:47,552 --> 00:20:49,732
I think that's also the European sensibility.

205
00:20:50,638 --> 00:20:56,164
but we got to embrace the ambition and the optimism that this will work out.

206
00:20:56,430 --> 00:20:57,952
Like just don't only focus.

207
00:20:58,032 --> 00:21:00,272
It's very easy to just focus on the negatives, right?

208
00:21:00,352 --> 00:21:02,272
Like, oh, this could go wrong, this could go wrong.

209
00:21:02,510 --> 00:21:03,396
Yeah, but it might not.

210
00:21:03,872 --> 00:21:08,352
And it's actually all up for grabs, in my opinion, what's going to happen from here.

211
00:21:08,654 --> 00:21:12,484
So we need more of us to get into the optimistic kind of space.

212
00:21:12,750 --> 00:21:17,636
And I think London specifically is great because of the ability to gather like this.

213
00:21:18,030 --> 00:21:28,036
So in the US, you know, if you're in finance, you're in New York, if you're in government, you're in DC, if you're in tech, you're in San Francisco, and if you're in entertainment, you're in LA.

214
00:21:28,366 --> 00:21:33,028
In London, we have a melting pot of one city that has all of that together.

215
00:21:33,358 --> 00:21:47,152
So if multidisciplinary, and if you believe my, I'd think thought of like multidisciplinary is important, I think London is the most multidisciplinary city in the world, where you can talk to world-leading experts in science, architecture, art, all in one place.

216
00:21:47,566 --> 00:21:54,980
You know, and again, this is I think something the RSA and the Royal Society and the Royal Academy of Engineering can can use as convening power.

217
00:21:55,312 --> 00:21:59,844
Um, and I don't think you can do that that easily anywhere else in the world at the at the highest level.

218
00:22:01,742 --> 00:22:03,792
talking to Demis makes you feel better about the future.

219
00:22:04,992 --> 00:22:11,232
Um, okay, I know loads of you will have tons of questions that you want to ask Demis, which I may get you to hold off for on the drinks.

220
00:22:11,312 --> 00:22:13,872
We have one last question from Usha.

221
00:22:13,952 --> 00:22:14,404
Where are you, Usha?

222
00:22:18,112 --> 00:22:20,672
I'm Usha, I'm an independent member of the House of Lords.

223
00:22:20,832 --> 00:22:28,228
Can I first of all say thank you very much indeed for a very inspiring, encouraging, and optimistic uh talk?

224
00:22:28,672 --> 00:22:33,392
And I like the way you framed it, because in terms of the challenges and so on.

225
00:22:33,806 --> 00:22:36,592
But can I ask a rather practical question?

226
00:22:37,232 --> 00:22:42,276
Because you see the potential of how we can harness AI in a very positive sense.

227
00:22:42,894 --> 00:23:01,540
But what I'm concerned about is that how do we ensure that we actually infuse our education system in a way which talks about it very positively and harnesses the potential, because there's a great deal of talk about what is going to the cognitive development of children and so on.

228
00:23:01,870 --> 00:23:11,140
But it'd be very useful to hear from you, the framing, and what do we do now to ensure we actually realize the potential the way you've been talking about it?

229
00:23:12,814 --> 00:23:14,512
Yeah, that's a really important question.

230
00:23:14,752 --> 00:23:21,712
Um, so I don't know if you're gonna, you know, like my answer, but I I I think something pretty radical has to happen with education.

231
00:23:21,902 --> 00:23:22,752
That's the first thing.

232
00:23:22,958 --> 00:23:24,192
Maybe it needed to anyway.

233
00:23:24,352 --> 00:23:30,532
I think, you know, again, we're talking about a 50 year old post sort of war system that I think needs updating anyway.

234
00:23:30,992 --> 00:23:40,960
Um You know, my experience with school and in the 90s, and my my parents did some teaching as well later in their careers of kids that were behind.

235
00:23:41,294 --> 00:23:45,572
So they used to teach evening classes for kids that were behind and ahead at school.

236
00:23:46,062 --> 00:23:49,412
And the problem with the school system today is it caters for the sort of medium.

237
00:23:49,710 --> 00:23:53,520
So if you're if you're not in that, then you're you're sort of not really catered for.

238
00:23:54,010 --> 00:24:00,910
The smart kids, the talented kids, are not pushed enough, and then they get distracted, and then the kids behind, they're not helped enough.

239
00:24:01,360 --> 00:24:10,820
So I think the other thing is, you know, again, this goes back to my answer to Thomas about we've got to, this is happening, the AI revolution.

240
00:24:11,150 --> 00:24:12,560
That that can't be stopped, right?

241
00:24:12,654 --> 00:24:13,412
It's like the tide.

242
00:24:13,614 --> 00:24:16,880
There's just it's it's gonna happen, it's gonna work, you should assume that.

243
00:24:17,102 --> 00:24:19,556
But what isn't is shaping what it's gonna be.

244
00:24:19,918 --> 00:24:28,800
So either we can, you can, we as a country or we as a, you know, we as individuals, we can just sort of worry about the pessimistic things, try and hold the tide back.

245
00:24:28,974 --> 00:24:30,080
You're not gonna be successful.

246
00:24:30,160 --> 00:24:33,440
You know, China and US are gonna do it, whatever we do here, right?

247
00:24:33,550 --> 00:24:34,468
For for starters.

248
00:24:34,960 --> 00:24:37,604
But and then we it gets shaped for us.

249
00:24:38,320 --> 00:24:45,760
Or we can try and lean in and shape it the way that fits what we believe in and our values.

250
00:24:45,998 --> 00:24:48,800
And I think that's what everyone needs to get into that mindset.

251
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:49,680
That's the mindset I'm in.

252
00:24:49,760 --> 00:24:56,720
I've kind of made peace with it's going to be messy and it's not quite the way I planned it 20 years ago because it's got it became commercial earlier than I thought.

253
00:24:56,800 --> 00:25:04,772
I would have done more scientific and alpha fold type things first, and then gone to the more commercial chatbots, but it went, it what happened happened.

254
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:07,684
And we're going to get advances more quickly, but also the risks.

255
00:25:08,302 --> 00:25:18,212
So when coming to education, here again is like I think we should accept that we've got to, in my opinion, train our kids and our next generation to be native with AI.

256
00:25:18,382 --> 00:25:22,160
It would be like, you know, in the 90s, not training them on computers or the internet.

257
00:25:22,400 --> 00:25:24,900
They would be behind the rest of the world if you do that.

258
00:25:25,166 --> 00:25:31,940
And that's the only way they're going to then be fit, you know, have the tools and equipped with the skills to shape the future.

259
00:25:32,270 --> 00:25:37,732
But but we can't do that, we've got to do that in a very intentional way again, that helps with the learning.

260
00:25:38,160 --> 00:25:43,524
So my thought would be, recommendation would be is potentially invert the classroom.

261
00:25:43,886 --> 00:25:47,428
So the classroom doesn't become about rote learning anymore.

262
00:25:47,566 --> 00:25:52,720
You do that out of class with the help of AI tools, because I think that's exactly what AI tools are good for.

263
00:25:52,974 --> 00:25:54,820
And the AI tools could do it personalized.

264
00:25:55,246 --> 00:26:02,960
So if you need a bit more help with algebra or whatever it is, because you're finding that particular the AI tools knows that and it gives you more questions on that, right?

265
00:26:03,040 --> 00:26:05,508
So imagine everyone has a great personal tutor.

266
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:15,600
You could imagine people who are really good at lecturing or teaching materials, you just watch YouTube videos of the best math teacher in the country.

267
00:26:15,854 --> 00:26:22,160
Why does everyone have, you know, I've got lots of friends of mine who are math teachers, and each of them having to produce the same class materials.

268
00:26:22,574 --> 00:26:24,228
This doesn't make sense to me, right?

269
00:26:24,400 --> 00:26:26,788
Uh, and and it's using up a lot of teacher time.

270
00:26:27,150 --> 00:26:28,836
So then what do you do in the classroom?

271
00:26:29,040 --> 00:26:42,276
That should be more like Montessori, project-based, human-human, all the things we, you know, thinking about could be important for the future: creativity, entrepreneurialism, um, working as a team, leading teams, being part of a team.

272
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:48,080
Well, how, you know, those things I think could be really amazing and fun to be done in school.

273
00:26:48,160 --> 00:26:50,404
And I think the teachers would prefer that, probably.

274
00:26:50,800 --> 00:26:52,880
And and just think about it really differently.

275
00:26:52,960 --> 00:27:00,160
It's a it's a collaborative space and a project space, like you maybe get in Oxbridge with like the supervision system, something like that.

276
00:27:00,366 --> 00:27:03,204
But bring that you know, or maybe Montessori schools.

277
00:27:03,470 --> 00:27:06,160
I didn't go to Montessori schools, I don't know exactly how it feels.

278
00:27:06,414 --> 00:27:09,892
But that type of approach, I think we need to explore those things.

279
00:27:10,926 --> 00:27:17,444
Well, ladies and gentlemen, we're gonna run over by a few minutes, but I'm sure nobody minds because it has been absolutely fascinating to hear from you, Demis.

280
00:27:17,760 --> 00:27:20,720
Please join me in giving one last round of applause for Democrats.

